    "title": "Balanced Crystalloids versus Saline in Critically Ill Adults",
    "citation": "Semler MW, Self WH, Wanderer JP, and others. Balanced Crystalloids versus Saline in Critically Ill Adults. New England Journal of Medicine. 2018;378(9):829-839.",
    "pmid": "29485925",
    "doi": "10.1056/NEJMoa1711584",
    "design": "Pragmatic, unblinded, cluster-randomized, multiple-crossover trial in five ICUs at one academic center. The primary outcome was a major adverse kidney event within 30 days, a composite of death, new renal-replacement therapy, or persistent renal dysfunction (final inpatient creatinine at least 200% of baseline), censored at hospital discharge or 30 days, whichever came first.",
    "population": "Adults 18 years of age or older admitted to a participating ICU. In all, 15,802 patients from five ICUs were enrolled, 7942 assigned to balanced crystalloids and 7860 assigned to saline.",
