    "comparator": "0.9% sodium chloride whenever intravenous isotonic crystalloid was administered.",
    "primaryResult": "A major adverse kidney event within 30 days occurred in 1139 patients (14.3%) in the balanced-crystalloids group and 1211 patients (15.4%) in the saline group (marginal odds ratio, 0.91; 95% CI, 0.84 to 0.99; conditional odds ratio, 0.90; 95% CI, 0.82 to 0.99; P=0.04). The balanced-crystalloids group comprised 7942 patients and the saline group 7860 patients.",
    "safety": "In-hospital death before discharge and within 30 days occurred in 818 patients (10.3%) in the balanced-crystalloids group and 875 patients (11.1%) in the saline group (P=0.06). New renal-replacement therapy occurred in 189 patients (2.5%) in the balanced-crystalloids group and 220 patients (2.9%) in the saline group (P=0.08). Persistent renal dysfunction occurred in 6.4% of the balanced-crystalloids group and 6.6% of the saline group (P=0.60). A plasma chloride concentration greater than 110 millimoles per liter occurred in 24.5% of the balanced-crystalloids group versus 35.6% of the saline group (P<0.001). A plasma bicarbonate concentration less than 20 millimoles per liter occurred in 35.2% of the balanced-crystalloids group versus 42.1% of the saline group (P<0.001).",
    "takeaway": "Among critically ill adults, balanced crystalloids for intravenous fluid administration resulted in a lower rate of a major adverse kidney event within 30 days than saline (14.3% versus 15.4%; marginal odds ratio, 0.91; 95% CI, 0.84 to 0.99; P=0.04).",
    "limitations": "Conduct at a single academic center limits generalizability, and treating clinicians were aware of the assigned crystalloid and of the ICU assignment sequence. Patients who remained in the ICU across a calendar-month change could be exposed to both crystalloids, and patients could be enrolled again on a later ICU admission. Censoring at hospital discharge may underestimate death at 30 days and overestimate persistent renal dysfunction at 30 days. Lactated Ringer's solution and Plasma-Lyte A were evaluated together.",
